- op: discuss  # a65a
  at: '2026-03-20T15:34:04Z'  # a65a
  by: agent  # a65a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a65a
  clock: 8  # a65a
  nonce: a65a  # a65a
  message: "Not necessarily a full language server — but something between what we have now and full type resolution.\n\nCurrently, IO boundary matching is purely name-based: if a call edge's callee is named 'Read', the catalog checks if any IO primitive is named 'Read'. The Go catalog has 'net.Conn.Read' as a network primitive, so *any* .Read() call matches — including crypto/rand.Reader.Read(), bufio.Reader.Read(), bytes.Buffer.Read(), etc.\n\nThe fix doesn't need full LSP-style type inference. It needs the Go analyzer to record *what type the receiver is* when it creates a call edge. The Go analyzer already does this in some cases — that's how typed_receiver_call edges work (confidence 0.85). The gap is specifically for variables whose type comes from:\n- Range loops: `for _, p := range plugins` (p's type is the slice element type)\n- Interface variables from function parameters that aren't directly typed\n- Variables assigned from function returns\n\nThree possible approaches, in order of complexity:\n\n1. **Module-qualified matching** (simplest): Instead of matching bare 'Read', require the catalog match to include the module. 'net.Conn.Read' only matches calls where the import path includes 'net'. This eliminates most false positives without any type inference. The Go analyzer already tracks import paths for many calls.\n\n2. **Propagate type from context** (medium): Extend the existing typed_receiver_call logic to cover range loops and more assignment patterns. The Go analyzer already resolves types for direct parameter types — extending it to range element types is incremental.\n\n3. **Multi-fidelity analysis** (ADR-0012): Full type-aware resolution where a high-fidelity pass uses type information from go/types or similar. This would fix the problem completely but is a much larger project.\n\nOption 1 would eliminate most false positives with minimal implementation effort. The current Go IO catalog already has module-qualified entries (net.Conn.Read, os.File.Read) — we just don't enforce the module constraint during matching."  # a65a
- op: discuss  # ac73
